/spec new NetMaker custom json-render ui
Thought Process\n  Clarifying Kiro Specs\n  Here is a prompt for Kiro to execute its spec workflow (.kiro/specs/) to build a custom json-render.dev Schema UI renderer that encompasses the gRPC and\n  socket networking surfaces across OP-DBUS.\n  ──────\n  ### Kiro Execution Prompt\n    # TASK PROMPT FOR KIRO SPEC WORKFLOW: Custom json-render Schema UI for gRPC & Socket Networking Surfaces\n    \n    Execute your standard Kiro spec workflow to plan, design, and implement a custom `json-render` Schema UI renderer in OP-DBUS (`crates/op-web` /\n  `crates/zeroclaw-gui`) that exposes the complete gRPC and socket networking surface area added to the control plane.\n    \n    Target Spec Directory: `.kiro/specs/jsonrender-grpc-socket-surface/`\n    Required Files to Generate: `requirements.md`, `design.md`, `tasks.md`, `spec.md`\n    \n    ---\n    \n    ## CONTEXT & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS\n    \n    ### 1. Catalog & Sealed SHM Blobs (SSOT)\n    - **Canonical SHM Path**: `/dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/` (`OPBLOB01` format binary files).\n    - **Blob Structure**: 64-byte header (`OPBLOB01`, section count, SHA256 of section 1) followed by section tables:\n      - **Section 1**: Canonical `PluginSchema` JSON (derives state structs + method signatures via `schemars`).\n      - **Section 2**: `BlobManifest`.\n      - **Section 3**: `FileDescriptorSet` (protobuf reflection descriptor payload synthesized from `PluginSchema.methods`).\n    - **Change Detection**: Read `catalog_hash` + `generation` from `.manifest.json`.\n    \n    ### 2. gRPC Surface Area\n    - **Endpoints**:\n      - Native Unix Socket: `/run/ghostbridge/grpc.sock` (owned by `op-grpc-bridge`).\n      - gRPC-Web TCP backend: `127.0.0.1:8090`.\n      - External Xray listener / proxy: `188.68.58.237:8090` / `127.0.0.1:10809`.\n    - **Reflection**: `tonic-reflection` v1 and v1alpha mounted, exposing `PerMethodGrpcServices` dynamic descriptors.\n    - **RPC Lifecycle**: Dynamic unary RPC execution, streaming response handling, and reflection-based schema rendering.\n    \n    ### 3. Socket Networking & Infrastructure Surface Area\n    - **Unix Domain Sockets**: `/run/ghostbridge/grpc.sock`, `/run/opdbus/session-bus.sock`, `/run/openvswitch/db.sock`, `/run/rust-network-manager/rust-\n  network-manager.sock`.\n    - **TCP Services & Container Listeners**: Netmaker API (`8081`), EMQX (`1883`, `8083`, `8883`), Rust Network Manager health (`9100`), Proxy (`3128`).\n    - **Network Topology**: OVS bridge (`ovsbr0`) and physical adapter (`eth0`) state telemetry, Xray routing states, WireGuard client status.\n    \n    ### 4. Policy Constraints (Mandatory)\n    - Host services MUST be managed via `sudo sv <command> <service>` (runit PID 1).\n    - Container app lifecycle MUST use D-Bus via `busctl`.\n    - Xray live config MUST exist ONLY at `/etc/xray/xray_config.json` inside the container.\n    - `json-render` output format MUST adhere to catalog components (`root`, `data`, `actions`). Never invent non-catalog props or actions.\n    \n    ---\n    \n    ## WORKFLOW STEPS TO EXECUTE\n    \n    ### Phase 1: Create `requirements.md`\n    Define non-functional and functional requirements covering:\n    1. **REQ-1 (SHM Blob Schema & Proto Extraction)**: Parser and reader for `OPBLOB01` Section 1 (`PluginSchema`) and Section 3 (`FileDescriptorSet`).\n    2. **REQ-2 (gRPC Reflection & Execution Surface)**: Interactive dynamic gRPC method invocation widgets, streaming gRPC response visualizers, and gRPC\n  reflection tree navigation.\n    3. **REQ-3 (Socket & Network Topology Surface)**: Live telemetry widgets for Unix domain socket availability, TCP port health indicators, and OVS bridge\n  state visualizations.\n    4. **REQ-4 (json-render Component Catalog Expansion)**: Define formal `json-render` UI DSL schemas for network components:\n       - `GrpcMethodCaller`, `GrpcStreamViewer`, `ReflectionTreeExplorer`\n       - `SocketStatusPill`, `TcpHealthBadge`, `NetworkTopologyGraph`\n       - `SchemaFormBuilder` (derived directly from `schemars` schemas)\n    5. **REQ-5 (Action Dispatching & Transport Layer)**: Handlers for `json-render` action types (`grpc.call`, `grpc.stream_subscribe`, `socket.\n  check_health`, `schema.mutate`).\n    \n    ### Phase 2: Create `design.md`\n    Detail the architecture and technical mechanics:\n    - **Rust/TypeScript Data Flow Architecture**: Component layout, bridge state management, and schema-to-UI interpreter pipeline in `op-web`/`zeroclaw-\n  gui`.\n    - **JSON Schemas**: Full JSON Schema declarations for all newly introduced `json-render` components, properties, and action payloads.\n    - **Security & Validation Layer**: OSCAL subid tracking (`x-oscal-subid`), input sanitization, and D-Bus/gRPC policy validation.\n    - **Failover & Error Handling**: Graceful degradations when Unix sockets are unavailable or `OPBLOB01` catalog is re-indexing.\n\n    ### Phase 3: Create `tasks.md`\n    Decompose the implementation into atomic, verifiable tasks:\n    1. Core SHM blob section extraction and reflection reader (`crates/op-blob`, `crates/op-web`).\n    2. Frontend catalog extensions (`dsl.rs`, `interpret.rs` in `zeroclaw-gui` / `op-web`).\n    3. Dynamic gRPC & Unix socket action handlers (bridge connection to `/run/ghostbridge/grpc.sock` and `127.0.0.1:8090`).\n    4. Schema UI visualizer components (gRPC stream viewer, socket network status, topology graphs).\n    5. Integration tests and spec validation (`cargo test`, `grpcurl` reflection verification).\n\n    ### Phase 4: Create `spec.md`\n    Consolidate the spec overview, target commit artifacts, API contracts, and acceptance criteria.\n\n    ---\n\n    ## ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n    - Full `.kiro/specs/jsonrender-grpc-socket-surface/` directory with all 4 markdown documents populated cleanly.\n    - `json-render` catalog fully specifies gRPC dynamic execution, gRPC streaming, socket endpoint monitoring, and network topology visualization.\n    - Strict compliance with `OPBLOB01` sealed blob contract and host runit service management rules.\n    ──────\n  ### How Kiro Will Process This Prompt\n\n  When given this prompt, Kiro will execute its specification workflow in 4 distinct phases:\n\n  1. requirements.md Generation: Establishes functional requirements (SHM extraction, gRPC reflection, Unix/TCP socket telemetry, json-render catalog\n  expansion).\n  2. design.md Generation: Details the architectural data flow from /dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/ to op-web/zeroclaw-gui, mapping the dynamic UI interpreter\n  and action handlers.\n  3. tasks.md Generation: Breaks down the implementation step-by-step with clean verification gates.\n  4. spec.md Generation: Provides the unified executive spec definition for execution.
